1
00:00:02,300 --> 00:00:06,010
Here's a smeltr demo fleet with two problems to fix.

2
00:00:06,010 --> 00:00:10,090
The guide up top walks us through it, one step at a time.

3
00:00:10,690 --> 00:00:15,110
In smeltr, nothing reaches the live fleet until it's deployed,

4
00:00:15,110 --> 00:00:17,400
so every fix starts on a branch.

5
00:00:21,970 --> 00:00:24,400
We'll call this one "fix-network".

6
00:00:24,400 --> 00:00:29,900
From here on, everything we save lands on this branch, not on what's running.

7
00:00:30,710 --> 00:00:33,210
Start the branch, and then open it.

8
00:00:37,920 --> 00:00:40,130
That chime marks step one done.

9
00:00:40,130 --> 00:00:43,490
The guide checks off each step as we finish it.

10
00:00:44,090 --> 00:00:47,660
With our branch open, let's find out what's wrong.

11
00:00:51,760 --> 00:00:54,260
Opening Status checks off step two.

12
00:00:54,260 --> 00:00:57,120
Status, then Issues, lists two problems.

13
00:00:57,120 --> 00:00:59,470
No route took cisco-asa's stream,

14
00:00:59,470 --> 00:01:04,330
and in fortigate-traffic, proto failed to read as its declared type.

15
00:01:07,100 --> 00:01:11,820
Let's take Cisco first, and open the Map to see where its logs go.

16
00:01:15,760 --> 00:01:20,540
On the Map, cisco-asa's stream is marked unrouted: its logs arrive,

17
00:01:20,540 --> 00:01:22,340
but nothing takes them.

18
00:01:24,270 --> 00:01:26,480
We add a route for that stream.

19
00:01:28,910 --> 00:01:33,060
There's no pipeline for it yet, so we choose New pipeline.

20
00:01:34,890 --> 00:01:37,680
The new pipeline starts with a capture.

21
00:01:37,680 --> 00:01:43,030
Press Start, and smeltr takes a sample of what cisco-asa is really sending.

22
00:01:43,030 --> 00:01:45,960
Then we save the sample to build against.

23
00:01:48,500 --> 00:01:53,930
smeltr reads all 100 records as Cisco ASA, so it proposes a parser for them.

24
00:01:53,930 --> 00:01:59,430
We open it to check: it splits each line into priority, time, host, severity,

25
00:01:59,430 --> 00:02:01,860
event code and the message itself.

26
00:02:05,730 --> 00:02:08,660
Opening a row shows each field filled in.

27
00:02:09,980 --> 00:02:12,120
Then we save the new pipeline.

28
00:02:17,030 --> 00:02:19,740
Saved, and the chime marks step three.

29
00:02:19,740 --> 00:02:23,670
The route now sends cisco-asa through its new pipeline.

30
00:02:25,030 --> 00:02:28,240
Save the route too, and step four checks off.

31
00:02:32,600 --> 00:02:35,740
That fixes the first problem on fix-network.

32
00:02:35,740 --> 00:02:37,740
The Map still says unrouted,

33
00:02:37,740 --> 00:02:40,100
because it shows what is running,

34
00:02:40,100 --> 00:02:42,100
and nothing is deployed yet.

35
00:02:42,910 --> 00:02:44,710
Now FortiGate.

36
00:02:44,710 --> 00:02:48,780
Its issue says proto failed to read as its declared type.

37
00:02:48,780 --> 00:02:51,350
We capture fresh records to see why.

38
00:02:52,710 --> 00:02:56,130
To capture new records, we open the Capture tab.

39
00:03:00,230 --> 00:03:03,020
We pick the fortigate-firewalls source.

40
00:03:06,220 --> 00:03:08,720
Records arrive as the capture runs.

41
00:03:09,650 --> 00:03:12,300
Then we save the capture as a sample.

42
00:03:13,190 --> 00:03:16,260
Now we can see there are 4 failing records.

43
00:03:17,070 --> 00:03:20,710
Click failing to filter down to just these records.

44
00:03:22,160 --> 00:03:26,370
Then we can see the error message under the row: in Step 2,

45
00:03:26,370 --> 00:03:30,230
the parser found that body.proto has the string "tcp",

46
00:03:30,230 --> 00:03:32,870
and could not parse it as an integer.

47
00:03:33,680 --> 00:03:39,040
We pause the records after this step, which shows the record as it is here.

48
00:03:39,640 --> 00:03:44,420
We show the whole raw line, and scroll down until proto is in view.

49
00:03:50,090 --> 00:03:53,660
And we confirm proto equals "tcp" in the raw line.

50
00:03:54,900 --> 00:03:58,320
Then back to the whole pipeline, with every row.

51
00:04:01,070 --> 00:04:04,500
The fix is to declare proto as a string instead,

52
00:04:04,500 --> 00:04:07,850
so it can hold a name such as tcp, or a number.

53
00:04:09,070 --> 00:04:11,210
We set proto's type to string.

54
00:04:14,970 --> 00:04:18,190
Saving the pipeline catches what that breaks.

55
00:04:18,190 --> 00:04:24,190
network.transport compares proto to the numbers 6 and 17, the codes for tcp and udp,

56
00:04:24,190 --> 00:04:26,040
and now proto is a string,

57
00:04:26,040 --> 00:04:28,610
which can't be compared to a number.

58
00:04:28,610 --> 00:04:31,540
So we compare to "6" and "17" as strings,

59
00:04:31,540 --> 00:04:34,540
and pass anything else through as it came,

60
00:04:34,540 --> 00:04:38,540
so downstream keeps the name instead of calling it icmp.

61
00:04:47,270 --> 00:04:49,700
Then we lock the output as a test.

62
00:04:49,700 --> 00:04:54,560
If a later edit changes what this pipeline produces, the test fails.

63
00:04:55,360 --> 00:04:57,930
We name the test for what it checks.

64
00:05:05,470 --> 00:05:09,390
Both fixes are on the branch, so we open it for review.

65
00:05:09,390 --> 00:05:13,820
The title we give is how this change is known once it deploys.

66
00:05:16,560 --> 00:05:19,780
smeltr drafts the description from the saves.

67
00:05:24,030 --> 00:05:28,530
Comments sit on the change, right beside every save it carries,

68
00:05:28,530 --> 00:05:31,950
so a reviewer sees the whole story in one place.

69
00:05:35,000 --> 00:05:39,860
In the demo, we play the reviewer too, so we approve our own change.

70
00:05:39,860 --> 00:05:43,930
On a real fleet, a teammate can approve it instead of us.

71
00:05:48,030 --> 00:05:49,830
That's all seven steps.

72
00:05:49,830 --> 00:05:54,190
On a real fleet, a reviewer would approve this and deploy it,

73
00:05:54,190 --> 00:05:57,330
and Status would clear, with no issues left.

74
00:05:57,330 --> 00:05:59,620
The demo stops before deploying.
